x86_64-windows  a different refusal answers first here, and it is broader: a destructible local and a `try` in one function is refused function-wide for this target, body or handler, because a Microsoft cleanup is a funclet and a state in the FH3 tables rather than a row in a call-site list. So the shape this case measures - a destructible local inside a handler, which both Itanium targets compile since 2026-09-09 - is still refused here, and by the broader rule rather than by the one that was lifted. This file was a `.error` case until then; what it records now is a difference between the targets rather than a gap in all three. See try-body-local.notarget for the same lag stated from the other side.
